# UI/UX — layout and typography

`context/instructions/ui-layout.md` is the reference for arranging a surface and
setting type: grouping, spacing, reading order and the type scale. It is
project-agnostic — it names the decisions, not the numbers. Read it with
`context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine), `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`
(the values), `context/instructions/ui-taste.md` (composition and direction) and
`context/instructions/ui-components.md` (the pieces you arrange).

## When this applies

- Use it when arranging a surface or setting type. If the project has no user
  interface, this file does not apply: say so and stop.
- The project's own grid and scale win. If it has none, this file still applies
  as decisions to follow, but never invent a design system as a side effect (see
  `ui-tokens.md`).

## Layout is grouping

- Group related things, separate unrelated ones: proximity and spacing carry
  structure better than borders, boxes or dividers.
- A shared alignment line creates order; a divider is usually a missing spacing
  decision.
- Give each surface one clear focal point and let the layout express the
  hierarchy (see `ui-taste.md`).

## Spacing is a system, not taste

- Space from the project's scale (`ui-tokens.md`), not from arbitrary values;
  siblings share one rhythm, and a rhythm break marks a group break.
- Name container roles semantically — page, section, card, field — and space
  between roles, not between arbitrary wrappers.
- Whitespace is structure, not waste: when in doubt, remove a line and add space.

## Typography is a system

- Use a small, closed set of type roles (display, title, body, label, caption),
  each mapped to the project's scale; never set a size ad hoc.
- **Measure** — body text runs roughly 45-75 characters per line. Wider loses the
  line; narrower breaks the rhythm.
- Size, weight and line-height carry hierarchy; do not push all three at once for
  a single step. Tabular numerals for data (see `ui-components.md`).

## Reading order

- DOM order is the reading and focus order: the tab order follows the visual
  order, and the content makes sense with styles off.
- Do not reorder visually with CSS in a way that contradicts the DOM — a screen
  reader and a keyboard follow the DOM, not the layout. If a visual reorder needs
  a different reading order, fix the DOM instead.

## The squint test

- Blur the surface, or squint at it: if the hierarchy still reads, the layout is
  structured; if everything collapses into one mass, the grouping is missing.
- Run it at the real viewport, not a zoomed-out mock.

## Boundary

- Values come from `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`.
- Composition, direction and coherence live in
  `context/instructions/ui-taste.md`; component internals are
  `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
- The performance budgets the structure must respect are in
  `context/instructions/ui-performance.md`.
